<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<topic id="Connecting-To-A-Server" revisionNumber="1">
  <developerConceptualDocument
    xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5"
    xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">

    <introduction>
      <para>
        <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpClient</codeEntityReference>,
        <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Pop3.Pop3Client</codeEntityReference> and
        <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Imap.ImapClient</codeEntityReference> all derive from
        <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.MailService</codeEntityReference>, so connecting, securing the
        connection, authenticating, logging and error handling work the same way regardless of which
        protocol you are using. This topic covers those common features.
      </para>
      <alert class="tip">
        <para>
          Every method that performs network I/O has an <literal>Async</literal> counterpart (for example,
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.MailService.ConnectAsync</codeEntityReference>).
          The examples in this documentation mostly use the synchronous methods for brevity, but the
          asynchronous methods are recommended for UI and server applications.
        </para>
      </alert>
      <autoOutline />
    </introduction>

    <section address="SecureSocketOptions">
      <title>Choosing how to secure the connection</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          The <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SecureSocketOptions</codeEntityReference> argument passed to
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.MailService.Connect</codeEntityReference>
          determines whether and how the connection is encrypted using SSL/TLS:
        </para>
        <table>
          <tableHeader>
            <row>
              <entry><para>Value</para></entry>
              <entry><para>Behavior</para></entry>
            </row>
          </tableHeader>
          <row>
            <entry><para><codeInline>None</codeInline></para></entry>
            <entry><para>No SSL/TLS is used. Credentials are sent in the clear unless the SASL mechanism protects them.</para></entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para><codeInline>SslOnConnect</codeInline></para></entry>
            <entry><para>The connection is encrypted immediately (implicit TLS). Used with the SMTPS (465), IMAPS (993) and POP3S (995) ports.</para></entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para><codeInline>StartTls</codeInline></para></entry>
            <entry><para>
              Connects in plain text and then upgrades the connection using the STARTTLS (or STLS) command.
              An exception is thrown if the server does not support it. Typically used with ports 587, 143 and 110.
            </para></entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para><codeInline>StartTlsWhenAvailable</codeInline></para></entry>
            <entry><para>
              Like <codeInline>StartTls</codeInline>, but silently continues unencrypted if the server
              does not support STARTTLS. This is vulnerable to downgrade attacks.
            </para></entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para><codeInline>Auto</codeInline></para></entry>
            <entry><para>
              Uses <codeInline>SslOnConnect</codeInline> for the well-known SSL ports (465, 993 and 995)
              and <codeInline>StartTlsWhenAvailable</codeInline> for any other port.
            </para></entry>
          </row>
        </table>
        <alert class="warning">
          <para>
            When you know which ports and security options your server supports, prefer
            <codeInline>SslOnConnect</codeInline> or <codeInline>StartTls</codeInline> over
            <codeInline>Auto</codeInline> or <codeInline>StartTlsWhenAvailable</codeInline> so that a
            man-in-the-middle cannot silently downgrade the connection to plain text.
          </para>
        </alert>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ConnectionExamples.cs" region="ConnectWithStartTls"/>
        <para>
          After connecting, the <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.MailService.IsSecure</codeEntityReference>,
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.MailService.SslProtocol</codeEntityReference> and
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.MailService.SslCipherSuite</codeEntityReference> properties
          describe the negotiated TLS session:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\SmtpExamples.cs" region="SslConnectionInformation"/>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="TlsSettings">
      <title>Configuring SSL/TLS</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          The following properties can be set before connecting to customize the TLS handshake:
        </para>
        <list class="bullet">
          <listItem>
            <para>
              <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.MailService.SslProtocols</codeEntityReference>: the SSL/TLS
              protocol versions that are allowed. On .NET (Core), the default (<codeInline>SslProtocols.None</codeInline>)
              lets the operating system choose the best available version, which is usually what you want.
              On .NET Framework, the default is the value of <codeInline>ServicePointManager.SecurityProtocol</codeInline>.
            </para>
          </listItem>
          <listItem>
            <para>
              <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.MailService.CheckCertificateRevocation</codeEntityReference>:
              whether the server certificate's revocation status should be checked (the default is
              <codeInline>true</codeInline>).
            </para>
          </listItem>
          <listItem>
            <para>
              <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.MailService.ClientCertificates</codeEntityReference>: client
              certificates to present to servers that require mutual TLS.
            </para>
          </listItem>
          <listItem>
            <para>
              <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.MailService.SslCipherSuitesPolicy</codeEntityReference>: restricts the
              cipher suites that may be negotiated (only supported on some platforms).
            </para>
          </listItem>
        </list>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ConnectionExamples.cs" region="ConfigureTls"/>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="CertificateValidation">
      <title>Validating server certificates</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          By default, MailKit validates the server's certificate using the operating system's certificate
          store, just like a web browser. If validation fails, an
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SslHandshakeException</codeEntityReference> is thrown whose
          message explains why the certificate was rejected.
        </para>
        <para>
          To customize validation (for example, to accept a self-signed certificate for a specific
          internal server), set the
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.MailService.ServerCertificateValidationCallback</codeEntityReference>
          property before connecting. Be careful to only accept the specific certificate that you expect:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\SslCertificateValidation.cs"/>
        <alert class="warning">
          <para>
            Never use a callback that simply returns <codeInline>true</codeInline> in production code. Doing
            so disables all protection against man-in-the-middle attacks.
          </para>
        </alert>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="Proxies">
      <title>Connecting through a proxy</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          To connect through a proxy server, set the
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.MailService.ProxyClient</codeEntityReference> property to one of
          the proxy clients in the <codeEntityReference>N:MailKit.Net.Proxy</codeEntityReference> namespace:
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Proxy.HttpProxyClient</codeEntityReference>,
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Proxy.HttpsProxyClient</codeEntityReference>,
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Proxy.Socks4Client</codeEntityReference>,
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Proxy.Socks4aClient</codeEntityReference>,
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Proxy.Socks5Client</codeEntityReference> or
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Proxy.WebProxyClient</codeEntityReference>
          (which wraps a <codeEntityReference>T:System.Net.IWebProxy</codeEntityReference>).
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ProxyExamples.cs" region="ProxyClient"/>
        <para>
          The <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.MailService.LocalEndPoint</codeEntityReference> property can be used
          to bind the connection to a specific local IP address and/or port.
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="Timeouts">
      <title>Timeouts and cancellation</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          The <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.MailService.Timeout</codeEntityReference> property (2 minutes by
          default) limits how long any single network read or write may take. It does not limit the total
          duration of an operation; to do that, pass a <codeEntityReference>T:System.Threading.CancellationToken</codeEntityReference>.
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ConnectionExamples.cs" region="TimeoutsAndCancellation"/>
        <alert class="note">
          <para>
            Canceling a command that is in progress usually leaves the connection in an unknown state, so
            MailKit will disconnect. You will need to reconnect and re-authenticate before you can continue.
          </para>
        </alert>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="ProtocolLogging">
      <title>Logging the protocol</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          When something goes wrong, a protocol log is the most useful diagnostic tool available. Pass a
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.ProtocolLogger</codeEntityReference> to the client's constructor to
          log all of the commands and responses exchanged with the server:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\ImapExamples.cs" region="ProtocolLogger"/>
        <para>
          By default, <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.ProtocolLogger.RedactSecrets</codeEntityReference> is
          <codeInline>true</codeInline>, so passwords and authentication tokens are replaced with
          <literal>********</literal> in the log. Message contents are <legacyItalic>not</legacyItalic>
          redacted, so be careful when sharing logs. You can also implement
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.IProtocolLogger</codeEntityReference> yourself to route the output
          to your own logging framework.
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="Telemetry">
      <title>Metrics and tracing</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          On .NET 8 and later, MailKit can emit metrics and traces using
          <codeEntityReference>T:System.Diagnostics.Metrics.Meter</codeEntityReference> and
          <codeEntityReference>T:System.Diagnostics.ActivitySource</codeEntityReference>, which can be collected
          with OpenTelemetry. Enable them once at startup using the
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Telemetry</codeEntityReference> class:
        </para>
        <code language="c#">
Telemetry.SmtpClient.Configure ();
Telemetry.Pop3Client.Configure ();
Telemetry.ImapClient.Configure ();
        </code>
        <para>
          See
          <externalLink>
            <linkText>Telemetry.md</linkText>
            <linkUri>https://github.com/jstedfast/MailKit/blob/master/Telemetry.md</linkUri>
            <linkTarget>_blank</linkTarget>
          </externalLink>
          for the list of available metrics and traces.
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="Exceptions">
      <title>Handling errors</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          The most common exceptions thrown by MailKit are:
        </para>
        <list class="bullet">
          <listItem>
            <para>
              <codeEntityReference>T:System.Net.Sockets.SocketException</codeEntityReference>: the host name
              could not be resolved or the connection was refused.
            </para>
          </listItem>
          <listItem>
            <para>
              <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SslHandshakeException</codeEntityReference>: the TLS
              handshake failed, usually because the server certificate could not be validated or because
              the client and server have no SSL/TLS protocol version in common.
            </para>
          </listItem>
          <listItem>
            <para>
              <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.AuthenticationException</codeEntityReference>: the
              credentials were rejected.
            </para>
          </listItem>
          <listItem>
            <para>
              <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.CommandException</codeEntityReference> (and the protocol-specific
              subclasses <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpCommandException</codeEntityReference>,
              <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Pop3.Pop3CommandException</codeEntityReference> and
              <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Imap.ImapCommandException</codeEntityReference>): the server
              rejected a command. The connection is still usable.
            </para>
          </listItem>
          <listItem>
            <para>
              <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.ProtocolException</codeEntityReference> (and subclasses): the
              server sent an unexpected response or the connection was dropped. The client will have been
              disconnected and must reconnect.
            </para>
          </listItem>
          <listItem>
            <para>
              <codeEntityReference>T:System.IO.IOException</codeEntityReference>: a network error occurred,
              including when the <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.MailService.Timeout</codeEntityReference>
              elapses during a synchronous operation (asynchronous operations throw a
              <codeEntityReference>T:System.TimeoutException</codeEntityReference> instead). The client will
              have been disconnected.
            </para>
          </listItem>
        </list>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\SmtpExamples.cs" region="ExceptionHandling"/>
      </content>
    </section>

